$DIR/main.p:32:3: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is kept in a variable that outlives the pass of a loop made with goto, outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that runs this code now. The pointer is good only while the task
    cannot move to another processor, so it may be kept only in local variables
    and used only inside the region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts
    disabled, or an rt.Lock held). Here it is kept in a variable that outlives
    the pass of a loop made with goto, where it can be used after the region
    ends or by another task.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the variable by name inside the region, or copy the value out and keep
    the copy:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        total := ticks
        rt.EnablePreemption()
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:48:2: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is used outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that ran the code that took it. Here the pointer is used where the
    region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock
    held) is no longer held, so the task may have moved to another processor and
    the pointer reaches that processor's copy while another task or an interrupt
    handler uses it.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the pointer only inside the region it was taken in, or take the address
    again where it is needed
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:61:4: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is kept in a variable of the enclosing function, which outlives the call, outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that runs this code now. The pointer is good only while the task
    cannot move to another processor, so it may be kept only in local variables
    and used only inside the region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts
    disabled, or an rt.Lock held). Here it is kept in a variable of the
    enclosing function, which outlives the call, where it can be used after the
    region ends or by another task.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the variable by name inside the region, or copy the value out and keep
    the copy:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        total := ticks
        rt.EnablePreemption()
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:75:2: the access through a pointer to ticks uses the per-CPU variable ticks outside a region with interrupts disabled, which it needs because the interrupt handler timer also uses it
    ticks has one copy for each processor. This is an access through a pointer,
    which is not a lone store of a whole variable of at most a machine word, so
    the task could move to another processor in the middle of it and read one
    processor's copy and write another's. The interrupt handler timer also uses
    ticks, so an interrupt on this processor could run in the middle of it as
    well: it must be inside a region with maskable interrupts disabled (x86.Cli
    to x86.Sti, x86.SaveFlagsCli to x86.RestoreFlags, or an rt.Lock held), not
    just with preemption disabled. A lone load or store of a whole variable of
    at most a machine word is allowed anywhere.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the access inside a region with interrupts disabled:
        f := x86.SaveFlagsCli()
        // the access to ticks
        x86.RestoreFlags(f)
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)
